{#
  What the host renders for a surface whose plugins publish no SSR entry — the default
  a fresh surface serves.

  It carries BOTH ways a plugin can contribute, because both exist and neither is
  wrong:

    - Server-resolved views become islands, filtered by claims and availability before
      any markup exists. They are in the HTML, so they survive without JavaScript.
    - Client-registered views mount into #callora-app. A plugin that only ships a
      bundle needs no server-side contribution at all.

  mountSurface does both in one pass, so a surface can use either or both. App.vue
  carries its own empty state, which is why this page has none: two empty states on
  one screen say less than one.
#}
{% extends "@callora/layout/page.njk" %}

{% block page_content %}
{% block app_islands %}{{ callora_slot("surface.main") }}{% endblock %}

{#
  readSurfaceContext reads the app root's OWN dataset, not an ancestor's, so the
  attributes are repeated here rather than inherited from the body.
#}
{% block app_root %}
<div id="callora-app" {% include "@callora/utilities/context-attributes.njk" %}></div>
{% endblock %}
{% endblock %}
